<div class="header-band">
  <span class="header-eyebrow">Case study &middot; Kalispell TRT</span>
  <h1>The phone still rings at the desk.<br /><em>Nothing gets missed after it stops.</em></h1>
  <p>Kalispell TRT is a hormone therapy and peptide clinic in Kalispell, serving patients by telehealth across Montana. They wanted their own team answering the phone &mdash; not an AI standing in front of it. That's what got built.</p>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>What they needed</h2>
    <p class="cs-lede">A clinic's front desk isn't a call center. The people who answer are the same people checking patients in, and the calls that get missed are the ones that arrive while they're doing that &mdash; plus every call that comes in after close.</p>
    <p>The brief was specific, and it ruled out the obvious approach: <strong>staff answer first, always.</strong> An AI that picked up before a human did would have been the wrong build, however impressive the demo.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>What we built</h2>
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <div class="cs-card">
        <h3>Person-first routing</h3>
        <p>Conditional forwarding rings the staff phone for about twenty seconds. The AI picks up <em>only</em> when nobody can. Verified end to end with the clinic on the line during staffed hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-card">
        <h3>Booking that runs itself</h3>
        <p>Consults land on the real calendar with a confirmation text, 24-hour and 1-hour reminders, and a public booking widget that blocks overlapping slots so a double-booking isn't possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-card">
        <h3>A hard clinical boundary</h3>
        <p>The assistant handles front-desk work only &mdash; hours, location, what the clinic treats, booking, messages. Anything clinical is declined and routed to staff by design, and that scope is written into the contract, not just the prompt.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-card">
        <h3>Calls, texts, and chat in one place</h3>
        <p>The same front desk answers the phone line, SMS, and the website chat, so a patient who starts in one channel isn't asked to repeat themselves in another.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Three things that actually happened</h2>

    <div class="cs-moment">
      <div class="cs-moment-mark">01</div>
      <div>
        <h3>The AI declined a clinical question and handed it to staff</h3>
        <p>During the first live week, callers asked the assistant clinical questions. It refused to answer them from its own knowledge and routed the callers to staff &mdash; exactly the designed behavior. The questions showed up in the knowledge-base log as <em>unanswered</em>, and they were left that way on purpose, with the clinic's agreement.</p>
        <p class="cs-note">That's the part worth reading twice: the gap was left open deliberately. An AI front desk that quietly starts answering medical questions is a liability, not a feature.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-moment">
      <div class="cs-moment-mark">02</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Three requested changes went live the same afternoon</h3>
        <p>The clinic emailed three refinements at 2:00 PM &mdash; how the assistant pronounced the website, leading pricing answers with the free consult, and how it handled a lab-work question. All three were deployed, test-called, verified, and confirmed back to the clinic that same afternoon.</p>
        <p class="cs-note">No ticket queue. The person who built it is the person who changes it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-moment">
      <div class="cs-moment-mark">03</div>
      <div>
        <h3>The owner booked a real patient himself, untrained, and got it right first try</h3>
        <p>Within the first week, Jason booked a patient consultation directly in the calendar without being walked through it, then messaged to ask whether he'd done it correctly. He had &mdash; right calendar, appointment confirmed, telehealth link attached. The confirmation text reached the patient within seconds, the reminders armed themselves, and the public widget blocked the overlapping slot.</p>
        <p class="cs-note">A system the owner can drive on instinct is worth more than one that needs its builder in the room.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

